/*
TODO(JOE): flash_attention_prefill

Purpose. Prefill today runs the decode kernel (paged_attention_decode_v2, or the split version) with one row per prompt token: row t walks its whole context of t + 1 tokens through its own copy of the block table, one thread block per (row, query head), and the 8 query heads that share a kv head each read it separately. The K and V bytes a prompt of L tokens reads therefore grow with L squared, and none of that work uses the tensor cores. #120 measured what it costs (results/prefill/prefill.md, commit 1693c46, single request, median of 5): engine TTFT 15.7, 97.8, 337.0 and 1,152.2 ms at 128, 512, 1,024 and 2,048 prompt tokens, against llama.cpp at 16.1, 44.0, 95.0 and 179.5 ms (-ub 512); per prompt token the engine goes from 0.123 to 0.563 ms while llama.cpp stays near 0.09. Those are the "before" numbers. This function is the FlashAttention-style replacement for the prefill rows: a causal kernel that loads a tile of queries once, streams tiles of keys and values past it, computes Q K^T and P V with mma on the tensor cores, keeps an online softmax per query row, and reads the chunk's own keys and values straight from the qkv buffer instead of the paged cache. The decode kernels stay as they are for decode rows.

Interface. Declared in include/engine/kernels/flash_prefill.h. One call handles every prefill chunk of a step (a step from Scheduler::schedule_chunked, laid out by build_chunked_batch: the decode rows first, then each chunk's rows in order, contiguous). qkv is the BF16 [T, (heads + 2 * kv_heads) * head_dim] output of the fused QKV GEMM after RoPE for all T rows of the step, and out is BF16 [T, heads * head_dim]. k_cache and v_cache are the BF16 [num_blocks, kv_heads, block_size, head_dim] views of the paged cache of this layer. chunk_starts is I32 [C + 1] and cached_lens is I32 [C], for C >= 0 chunks: chunk c is rows chunk_starts[c] to chunk_starts[c + 1] - 1 of qkv and out, and those rows are the tokens at positions cached_lens[c] to cached_lens[c] + count - 1 of one sequence, where count = chunk_starts[c + 1] - chunk_starts[c]. block_tables is I32 [C, max_blocks], one row per chunk (not per token): position j of chunk c's sequence is in physical block block_tables[c][j / block_size] at offset j % block_size. Rows before chunk_starts[0] and from chunk_starts[C] on are not prefill rows (in a mixed step, the decode rows) and are left alone. heads and kv_heads come from the shapes as in the paged kernels: head_dim = k_cache.shape[3], kv_heads = k_cache.shape[1], heads = out.shape[1] / head_dim.

Semantics. For row r = chunk_starts[c] + i, query head h, the query is at position p = cached_lens[c] + i and attends to positions 0 to p. Keys and values at positions below cached_lens[c] (the part of the sequence earlier steps already cached) come from the paged cache through block_tables[c]. Keys and values at positions cached_lens[c] to p come from the K and V columns of qkv rows chunk_starts[c] to r. In the engine write_kv has already copied those rows into the cache, but the kernel must not read the cache slots of positions at or after cached_lens[c]: the tests fill them with other values and with NaN. Query head h reads kv head h / (heads / kv_heads). out[r][h * head_dim + d] = sum over j <= p of P_j * V_j[d], with P the softmax over j <= p of s_j = scale * (q_h . K_j) and scale = 1 / sqrt(head_dim). qkv, both caches, block_tables, chunk_starts and cached_lens are not modified.

Numerics. Round each score as the naive and paged kernels do, s_j = bf16(bf16(q_h . K_j) * scale), with the dot product accumulated in FP32 (mma with FP32 accumulators does that; the order inside it is the hardware's). Keep the running maximum, the running sum and the output accumulator in FP32, rescale them with exp(m_old - m_new) when the maximum rises, and round the output to BF16 once after dividing by the sum. Rounding the unnormalized probabilities to BF16 to feed the P V mma is allowed. Masked positions (after p) contribute exactly nothing: no NaN from -inf - -inf, no 0 / 0 in a row whose first tile is entirely masked, and the result stays right when exp would overflow without subtracting the maximum (scores up to 128 in the tests). A query at position 0 with no cached prefix (cached_lens[c] = 0, i = 0) gives exactly V_0 for every head, bit for bit.

Determinism. The output of a row depends only on its own chunk: its query, the K and V rows of its chunk up to and including its own row, cached_lens[c], the first ceil(cached_lens[c] / block_size) entries of block_tables[c] and the cached tokens they reach. It is bit-identical whether the chunk is computed alone or next to other chunks and decode rows in the same call, whatever comes after the row in its own chunk (a chunk of 100 rows and the first 37 of those rows as a chunk of 37 give the same 37 outputs), and on every repeat. It does not have to be bit-identical across different chunkings of the same prompt (the same 300 tokens as one chunk, or as three chunks of 100 with the first two in the cache), only within the tolerance below. No atomics on floats, and nothing whose order depends on block timing.

Errors and preconditions. Before launching anything, throw std::invalid_argument with a message naming flash_attention_prefill when: k_cache or v_cache is not BF16 or not 4-dimensional, or their shapes differ; head_dim is not a multiple of 16 or is above 128 (the mma tiles need 16-wide k steps; this is the one limit the decode kernels do not have); out is not BF16 or not 2-dimensional, its width is not a multiple of head_dim, or heads is not a positive multiple of kv_heads; qkv is not BF16 or not 2-dimensional, its row count differs from out's, or its width is not (heads + 2 * kv_heads) * head_dim; chunk_starts is not I32, not 1-dimensional or empty; cached_lens is not I32, not 1-dimensional or not of length C; block_tables is not I32, not 2-dimensional, has a row count other than C or has no columns. The caller guarantees, without checks: 0 <= chunk_starts[0] <= chunk_starts[1] <= ... <= chunk_starts[C] <= T (a chunk may be empty); cached_lens[c] >= 0; cached_lens[c] + count <= max_blocks * block_size; and the first ceil(cached_lens[c] / block_size) entries of block_tables[c] are between 0 and num_blocks - 1 (later entries may be anything, -1 included). Those values live on the device, so the kernel cannot size its grid from them: no cudaMemcpy back to the host, no synchronization, no allocation. The call must be capturable in a CUDA graph (the tests capture it and replay it) and asynchronous on stream, with KERNEL_CHECK after each launch.

Edge cases. C = 0 (chunk_starts of length 1) and T = 0 are valid and launch nothing (the tensors of a T = 0 call may have null data). Empty chunks write nothing. A chunk of one row is a prefill of one token. Prompts of up to 8,192 tokens in one chunk, cached prefixes from 1 to several thousand tokens, chunk lengths on both sides of your tile sizes and of the block size, and C = 16 chunks of mixed lengths must all be right. Any positive block_size, any head_dim allowed above, and heads any multiple of kv_heads (the tests use head dims 16 to 128 and block sizes 1 to 32).

What to compare against. naive::attention on a contiguous copy of the chunk's context (the cached prefix followed by the chunk's own K and V) with start = cached_lens[c], within 1/64 of (the largest |V_j[d]| over j <= p) + |naive output| per element, and Hugging Face's attn output of every layer of the traced reference prompt within 2^-7 times the largest |V| of the visible tokens in that column, both as one chunk and as three chunks with the earlier ones cached. For speed: Nsight Systems on engine bench at 2,048 prompt tokens before and after, the attention share of prefill and its time per layer; Nsight Compute on the kernel alone (tensor pipe utilization, DRAM bytes read against the bytes of K and V the chunk actually needs, achieved occupancy, shared memory per block); then rerun the prefill section of scripts/reproduce.sh and put the new TTFT row next to the #120 numbers in docs/results.md. Record what tile sizes you tried and why you kept the ones you did in docs/profiling.md.

What is not here. Wiring is a separate Part B branch once the tests pass: PagedRunner sends prefill rows here and decode rows to v2 or split, build_chunked_batch emits chunk_starts, cached_lens and one table row per chunk (which also ends the per-token copy of the block table that #120 noted), and DecodeGraphs stays decode only.

Tests that prove it. tests/todo/gpu/test_flash_prefill.cpp (target todo_gpu_tests, labels todo_joe and gpu): one chunk of 37 tokens, chunk lengths around 16, 64 and 128, prompts of 2,048, 4,096 and 8,192 tokens, cached prefixes from 1 to 1,000 tokens, decode rows around the chunks left untouched with guard elements around out, 16 chunks in one call, empty chunks, scores up to 128, a running maximum that rises, falls or spikes, the first position equal to V bit for bit, NaN in the cache slots of the chunk's own positions and outside the context and -1 table entries leaving the output bit-identical, batch invariance, rows independent of the rows after them, repeatability, the same prompt chunked three ways, a graph capture and replay, six head and block layouts, HF's attn on all 22 layers as one chunk and as three, C = 0 and T = 0, and every rejected argument. Run them with ctest --preset debug -L todo_joe, then compute-sanitizer --tool memcheck on them. When they pass, move the file to tests/gpu/, add it to gpu_tests and delete todo_gpu_tests.
*/
void flash_attention_prefill(const Tensor&, const Tensor&, const Tensor&, const Tensor&,
                             const Tensor&, const Tensor&, const Tensor&, Stream) {
